WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.329 --> 00:00:02.770
INTRO:
It’s no secret that over the past decade,

00:00:02.770 --> 00:00:05.859
Warren Buffett hasn’t been at the top of
his game.

00:00:05.859 --> 00:00:10.660
In fact, between 2010 and 2020, he basically
just matched the market.

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:16.100
This isn’t terrible performance given that
most hedge funds don’t even match the market,

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:21.160
but for a legendary investor with an average
annual return of 20.5%, it’s rather disappointing.

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:27.330
This has caused many to wonder if Buffett
has lost his touch in the new digital world.

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:32.140
Despite this lackluster performance over the
past decade, Buffett has actually been crushing

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:33.330
it in 2022.

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:38.220
In fact, Berkshire Hathaway is slightly positive
on the year while the overall market has been

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:39.470
getting slaughtered.

00:00:39.470 --> 00:00:46.140
Currently, Berkshire Hathaway is up 3% on
the year while the S&amp;P 500 is down 16%.

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:50.480
This recent move means that Berkshire Hathaway
is officially outperforming the market not

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:55.650
just on an yearly basis, but since the bottom
of the 2008 financial recession which is when

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:57.560
he quote on quote “lost his edge”.

00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:02.060
So, here’s the top 5 reasons why Buffett
had been struggling to beat the market and

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:05.170
how he’s overcame these challenges.

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:10.510
VALUE STOCKS:
Likely one of the biggest reasons Buffett

00:01:10.510 --> 00:01:15.120
has had trouble beating the S&amp;P 500 is due
to his focus on value stocks.

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:20.320
In recent history, value stocks simply haven’t
kept up with growth stocks in terms of returns.

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:26.640
Between 2010 and 2020, growth stocks almost
outperformed value stocks by a full 100%.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:30.880
But, while growth stocks tend to outperform
during bull markets, they also get crushed

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:32.070
during bear markets.

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:36.610
And given that the NASDAQ is currently experiencing
a bear market, it’s not surprising that

00:01:36.610 --> 00:01:39.610
many of the high growth names have gotten
destroyed.

00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:43.450
Since the start of the year, the growth stocks
in the S&amp;P 500 have underperformed Berkshire

00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:45.840
Hathaway by about 25%.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:50.530
Conversely, many value stocks have performed
extremely well such as energy stocks.

00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:54.840
Since the start of the year, the value stocks
in the S&amp;P 500 have outperformed the overall

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:56.590
index by nearly 10%.

00:01:56.590 --> 00:02:01.460
So, in the current market environment, Buffett’s
value centric investing philosophy is holding

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:02.770
up pretty well.

00:02:02.770 --> 00:02:07.010
Aside from benefitting for a risk off market,
Buffett has also moved towards embracing a

00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:09.679
few high growth tech companies himself.

00:02:09.679 --> 00:02:14.310
If we take a look at his holdings, we’ll
see that Apple is by far his biggest position.

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:20.200
He currently has $133 billion in Apple which
accounts for nearly 40% of his entire portfolio.

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:24.140
Another mega cap company that Buffett has
recently embraced is Amazon.

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:28.840
His stake in Amazon isn’t nearly as large
as his stake in Apple, but it is still a billion

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:30.100
dollar position.

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:33.870
Buffett has even ventured into some more early
stage tech companies such as Snowflake which

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:35.690
isn’t even profitable yet.

00:02:35.690 --> 00:02:39.940
Currently, these stocks are actually holding
back Buffett given that they’re all down

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:41.480
by substantial amounts.

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:46.750
Apple is down 20%, Amazon is down 34%, and
Snowflake is down over 50%.

00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:50.820
So, if Buffett is able to post a positive
return with all these tech companies being

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:55.630
down so much, imagine what type of returns
he’ll be posting when these companies recover.

00:02:55.630 --> 00:02:59.690
In the end, Buffett’s willingness to finally
embrace tech while also maintaining his value

00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:03.400
positions has enabled him to enjoy the best
of both worlds.

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:08.760
TOO MUCH CASH:
Another major issue that Buffet has faced

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:11.819
over the past decade is simply putting his
money to work.

00:03:11.819 --> 00:03:15.600
Recently, Buffett has been holding significant
amounts of cash which of course produces little

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:17.040
to no returns.

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:21.849
His cash position has gotten slightly better
over the past 2 years as it has dropped from

00:03:21.849 --> 00:03:27.280
$128 billion to $106 billion today, but there’s
still a long way to go.

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:32.060
But, wait a minute, why is Buffett even having
trouble deploying his cash in the first place?

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:37.060
Well, at the core, Buffett is still a value
investor and though he has embraced some growth

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:40.360
plays, he’s not willing buy when prices
are quote “sky-high”.

00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:45.340
Now, you might disagree with this statement
given the recent sell off, but on a historical

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:48.120
basis, the market is still trading for a rich
valuation.

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:55.610
For example, the median PE ratio of S&amp;P 500
companies over the past 140 years is 14.89.

00:03:55.610 --> 00:04:01.640
Today, the PE ratio of the S&amp;P 500 stands
at 20.34 which means that the average company

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:04.030
is still overvalued by 33%.

00:04:04.030 --> 00:04:09.099
Similarly, the Buffett indicator which compares
the US stock market with the US GDP suggests

00:04:09.099 --> 00:04:11.459
that stocks are currently overvalued by 75%.

00:04:11.459 --> 00:04:15.849
Now, I’m sure there’s gonna be comments
down below that argue that these models are

00:04:15.849 --> 00:04:19.900
outdated due to low interest rates, excessive
money printing, and the rise of international

00:04:19.900 --> 00:04:21.350
markets.

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:25.930
While you may be right, we have to acknowledge
that Buffett is more of an old school investor.

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:29.889
So, I think its safe to assume that he gives
at least some weight to the indicator that

00:04:29.889 --> 00:04:32.009
was literally named after him.

00:04:32.009 --> 00:04:36.479
Alright, so the overall market may seem overvalued
from Buffett’s perspective.

00:04:36.479 --> 00:04:40.599
But, regardless of the market environment,
there’s always deals on the market right?

00:04:40.599 --> 00:04:45.719
Well, there are always deals on the market,
but the problem is that these are generally

00:04:45.719 --> 00:04:49.759
underrated small cap companies that won’t
really move the needle for Buffett.

00:04:49.759 --> 00:04:55.270
For example, let’s say you find an extraordinary
small cap company that’s worth $1 billion.

00:04:55.270 --> 00:04:59.410
Even if Buffett bought this entire company
and the company grew 10X within the next 10

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:01.319
years, he would only make $9 billion.

00:05:01.319 --> 00:05:06.099
And while that’s an insane amount of money,
it would only grow Buffett’s overall portfolio

00:05:06.099 --> 00:05:07.789
by a mere 2.6%.

00:05:07.789 --> 00:05:13.550
Considering this, investing in small cap companies
is simply not a feasible option for Buffett.

00:05:13.550 --> 00:05:17.819
He has to stick to companies that are worth
tens of billions if not hundreds of billions,

00:05:17.819 --> 00:05:21.569
and amongst these companies, it’s been extremely
difficult to spot a deal.

00:05:21.569 --> 00:05:25.219
One of the main ways that Buffett has been
combating this issue is buy constantly growing

00:05:25.219 --> 00:05:26.529
his existing positions.

00:05:26.529 --> 00:05:30.759
Last quarter, for example, he bought $600
million worth of Apple stock.

00:05:30.759 --> 00:05:34.879
But, this can only take him so far, and sooner
or later he’s going to have to find new

00:05:34.879 --> 00:05:36.860
companies to invest in.

00:05:36.860 --> 00:05:41.839
BECOMING THE MARKET:
As Buffett’s portfolio continues to grow

00:05:41.839 --> 00:05:46.309
and he concentrates on large cap companies,
one of the biggest hurdles he has to face

00:05:46.309 --> 00:05:48.669
is unintentionally becoming the market himself.

00:05:48.669 --> 00:05:55.580
I mean if you just keep adding large cap positions,
you basically just become the S&amp;P 500 yourself.

00:05:55.580 --> 00:05:59.909
Buffett currently has 48 positions in basically
every industry you can think of from tech

00:05:59.909 --> 00:06:02.460
and retail to food and banking.

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:06.749
While this may not sound like a big deal,
it is given that Buffett is currently breaking

00:06:06.749 --> 00:06:08.270
his own advice.

00:06:08.270 --> 00:06:12.210
One of Buffett’s most famous sayings is
that “Diversficiation is protection against

00:06:12.210 --> 00:06:13.210
ignorance.

00:06:13.210 --> 00:06:14.849
It makes little sense if you know what you’re
doing.”

00:06:14.849 --> 00:06:19.449
Now, I don’t think Buffett is diversifying
to protect against ignorance.

00:06:19.449 --> 00:06:23.659
I think he’s diversifying in an attempt
to deploy as much capital as possible.

00:06:23.659 --> 00:06:29.119
But, this naturally comes along with the unintended
consequence of limiting upside potential.

00:06:29.119 --> 00:06:33.210
Aside from breaking his own diversification
rule, Buffett is also actively breaking his

00:06:33.210 --> 00:06:34.529
20 slot rule.

00:06:34.529 --> 00:06:38.419
The rule suggests that an individual’s financial
success could be improved if they could only

00:06:38.419 --> 00:06:41.090
make 20 investments throughout their entire
life.

00:06:41.090 --> 00:06:45.399
The idea is that if an individual’s investment
choices are heavily constrained, they would

00:06:45.399 --> 00:06:48.099
be highly selective with each investment they
make.

00:06:48.099 --> 00:06:52.439
And hopefully, this selectiveness would improve
their chances of making good investments.

00:06:52.439 --> 00:06:56.219
Given that Buffett is currently holding onto
48 different positions, he’s clearly not

00:06:56.219 --> 00:06:57.479
abiding by this rule.

00:06:57.479 --> 00:07:02.819
But, this isn’t to say that Buffett isn’t
trying his best to limit diversification.

00:07:02.819 --> 00:07:07.770
Even though Buffett has dozens of positions,
his portfolio is still extremely top heavy.

00:07:07.770 --> 00:07:12.879
His top 10 positions, for example, account
for 85% of his portofolio and his top 20 positions

00:07:12.879 --> 00:07:15.430
account for 96% of his portfolio.

00:07:15.430 --> 00:07:19.919
In other words, all of Buffett’s investments
past the 20 slot rule only account for 4%

00:07:19.919 --> 00:07:20.999
of his portfolio.

00:07:20.999 --> 00:07:24.800
Despite all his efforts though, it’s only
natural that the performance of Berkshire

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:30.669
Hathaway gets closer and closer to that of
the market as they continue to grow and their

00:07:30.669 --> 00:07:32.300
investments mature.

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:36.569
MISSING THE MARK:
On top of all these external factors weighing

00:07:36.569 --> 00:07:40.780
on Berkshire’s performance, it should be
noted that Buffett has made a few major mistakes

00:07:40.780 --> 00:07:42.039
himself.

00:07:42.039 --> 00:07:47.229
For example, in the early 2010s, Buffett wanted
to get in on all the tech growth, so he decided

00:07:47.229 --> 00:07:50.360
to buy a legacy player which was IBM.

00:07:50.360 --> 00:07:56.529
In 2011, he announced that he purchased $10.7
billion of IBM at $170 per share.

00:07:56.529 --> 00:08:01.370
While IBM did reach about $200 per share in
2013, it has since been on a decline, and

00:08:01.370 --> 00:08:04.360
Buffett eventually ended up selling his shares
for a loss.

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:08.830
While this was no doubt a subpar investment,
Buffett also made one the worst investments

00:08:08.830 --> 00:08:12.770
of his career within the past decade which
is of course Kraft Heinz.

00:08:12.770 --> 00:08:17.029
While Buffett maintains that both Kraft and
Heinz were great companies fundamentally,

00:08:17.029 --> 00:08:20.659
he has admitted that he overpaid for the Kraft
portion of the business.

00:08:20.659 --> 00:08:24.249
Aside from paying a premium for the company,
Buffett went on to oversee a merger of the

00:08:24.249 --> 00:08:26.959
two companies which hasn’t been all that
successful.

00:08:26.959 --> 00:08:30.469
I have a full video on the downfall of Kraft-Heinz
if you’re interested.

00:08:30.469 --> 00:08:34.300
But in essence, the company couldn’t keep
up with the prices of generic brands or the

00:08:34.300 --> 00:08:36.340
healthniess of up and coming brands.

00:08:36.340 --> 00:08:40.940
So they’ve been stuck in this weird middle
ground that doesn’t appeal to either demographic.

00:08:40.940 --> 00:08:45.091
On top of this, Kraft-Heinz has been involved
in an accounting scandal, they’ve had to

00:08:45.091 --> 00:08:49.280
write off massive losses, and the CEO left
during the peak of their troubles.

00:08:49.280 --> 00:08:53.510
As you would guess, all of these issues have
resulted in Kraft-Heinz stock getting obliterated.

00:08:53.510 --> 00:08:57.740
They’re currently down over 50% from their
peak in 2017.

00:08:57.740 --> 00:09:02.680
Despite this horrendous performance, Buffett
has continued to vouch for Kraft-Heinz, but

00:09:02.680 --> 00:09:06.720
it should also be noted that he has been transparent
that he would’ve sold the stock a long time

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:10.320
ago if he was able to easily liquidate his
massive position.

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:14.510
Fortunately though, it does look like Kraft-Heinz
has finally bottomed out and it’s starting

00:09:14.510 --> 00:09:15.900
to form an upward trend.

00:09:15.900 --> 00:09:21.690
So, hopefully, Kraft-Heinz won’t weight
down on Buffett’s portfolio moving forward.

00:09:21.690 --> 00:09:25.880
SHIFTING PRIORITIES:
While everything we’ve covered so far in

00:09:25.880 --> 00:09:31.010
the video are valid points, something that
we shouldn’t overlook is Buffett’s age.

00:09:31.010 --> 00:09:33.340
Buffett is currently 91 years old.

00:09:33.340 --> 00:09:37.750
This isn’t to say that Buffett is too old
for this or anything like that, but it does

00:09:37.750 --> 00:09:41.990
bring up the question: How much does Buffett
even care about beating the market anymore?

00:09:41.990 --> 00:09:46.570
When you’re over 90 years old, I suspect
that you would care a lot more about living

00:09:46.570 --> 00:09:51.270
out the last of your days to the best of your
ability as opposed to beating some vain metric.

00:09:51.270 --> 00:09:54.650
Now, Buffett clearly still enjoys investing
and talking about stocks.

00:09:54.650 --> 00:09:58.710
That’s why he continues to lead Berkshire
and regularly participates in press events.

00:09:58.710 --> 00:10:02.990
But, with that being said, I think Buffett
has several priorities that are higher than

00:10:02.990 --> 00:10:05.310
beating the market like philanthropy.

00:10:05.310 --> 00:10:09.410
Warren Buffett is the biggest philanthropist
in the entire world having donated a massive

00:10:09.410 --> 00:10:11.470
$42.8 billion.

00:10:11.470 --> 00:10:16.260
And given that Buffett is looking to donate
all of his wealth after he dies, I suspect

00:10:16.260 --> 00:10:20.710
that his number 1 priority is to ensure that
all of his wealth ends up in the right hands.

00:10:20.710 --> 00:10:23.560
This aligns with what he’s been recommending
as well.

00:10:23.560 --> 00:10:27.450
After all, Buffett doesn’t even recommend
that you invest in Berkshire.

00:10:27.450 --> 00:10:30.500
He recommends that you invest in the S&amp;P 500
instead.

00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:33.800
Considering this, it’s pretty clear that
Buffett isn’t exactly trying to appeal to

00:10:33.800 --> 00:10:37.800
investors meaning that maximizing returns
is not his top priority.

00:10:37.800 --> 00:10:42.310
At this point, it seems like he’s just looking
to see how far he can take it while having

00:10:42.310 --> 00:10:43.490
some fun.

00:10:43.490 --> 00:10:46.640
And it just so happens, that he’s beating
the market once again.

00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:50.600
Do you guys think that this is just some random
fluke, or do you think Buffett will continue

00:10:50.600 --> 00:10:51.900
to lead the market?

00:10:51.900 --> 00:10:52.900
Comment that down below.

00:10:52.900 --> 00:10:56.790
Also, drop a like if you’re a fan of Buffett’s
insane philanthropy.

